(function(E){ const x = E["exam-04"] = E["exam-04"] || {units:{}}; (x.units = x.units || {})["x04-l4"] = {"id":"x04-l4","patterns":["L4-society","PARA-signpost"],"title":{"en":"Listening Part 4: a lecture on health","tr":"Listening Part 4: sağlık üzerine bir ders","fa":"Listening Part 4: سخنرانی‌ای درباره‌ی سلامت"},"objective":{"en":"In this lesson you will learn to complete ten notes during a lecture about health and hospitals, following the lecturer’s signposts and writing one word in the form you hear.","tr":"Bu derste sağlık ve hastaneler hakkındaki bir ders sırasında on notu, öğretim üyesinin yön gösteren ifadelerini takip ederek ve tek kelimeyi duyduğun biçimde yazarak tamamlamayı öğreneceksin.","fa":"در این درس یاد می‌گیری در طول سخنرانی‌ای درباره‌ی سلامت و بیمارستان ده یادداشت را کامل کنی، با دنبال کردن جمله‌های راهنمای استاد و نوشتن یک کلمه به همان شکلی که می‌شنوی."},"sessions":[["explain"],["drill"],["exit"]],"explain":{"title":{"en":"Listening Part 4: a lecture on health","tr":"Listening Part 4: sağlık üzerine bir ders","fa":"Listening Part 4: سخنرانی‌ای درباره‌ی سلامت"},"objective":{"en":"In this lesson you will learn to complete ten notes during a lecture about health and hospitals, following the lecturer’s signposts and writing one word in the form you hear.","tr":"Bu derste sağlık ve hastaneler hakkındaki bir ders sırasında on notu, öğretim üyesinin yön gösteren ifadelerini takip ederek ve tek kelimeyi duyduğun biçimde yazarak tamamlamayı öğreneceksin.","fa":"در این درس یاد می‌گیری در طول سخنرانی‌ای درباره‌ی سلامت و بیمارستان ده یادداشت را کامل کنی، با دنبال کردن جمله‌های راهنمای استاد و نوشتن یک کلمه به همان شکلی که می‌شنوی."},"stats":[{"label":{"en":"Questions","tr":"Soru","fa":"سؤال"},"value":"10"},{"label":{"en":"Speakers","tr":"Konuşan","fa":"گوینده"},"value":"1"},{"label":{"en":"Word limit","tr":"Kelime sınırı","fa":"سقف کلمه"},"value":{"en":"1 word","tr":"1 kelime","fa":"۱ کلمه"}}],"context":{"eyebrow":{"en":"1 The context","tr":"1 Bağlam","fa":"۱ بافت"},"head":{"en":"This exam: music in waiting rooms","tr":"Bu sınav: bekleme salonlarında müzik","fa":"این آزمون: موسیقی در اتاق انتظار"},"intro":{"en":"Part 4 is one academic lecture. Society, health and business are frequent subjects. A lecture on research usually moves from a problem, to findings, to practical advice, to limits and the future.","tr":"Part 4 tek bir akademik derstir. Toplum, sağlık ve iş dünyası sık çıkan konulardır. Araştırma üzerine bir ders genelde bir sorundan bulgulara, pratik önerilere, sınırlara ve geleceğe ilerler.","fa":"Part 4 یک سخنرانی دانشگاهی است. جامعه، سلامت و کسب‌وکار موضوع‌های پرتکرارند. سخنرانی درباره‌ی پژوهش معمولاً از یک مشکل به یافته‌ها، بعد توصیه‌های عملی، محدودیت‌ها و آینده می‌رود."},"technique":{"en":"2 The technique","tr":"2 Teknik","fa":"۲ تکنیک"},"labels":{"asks":{"en":"What this part is about","tr":"Bu kısım ne hakkında","fa":"این بخش درباره‌ی چیست"},"fields":{"en":"Headings you will see","tr":"Göreceğin başlıklar","fa":"عنوان‌هایی که می‌بینی"},"words":{"en":"Words that carry the answers","tr":"Cevabı taşıyan kelimeler","fa":"کلمه‌هایی که جواب را می‌آورند"},"a":{"en":"On the paper","tr":"Kâğıtta","fa":"روی برگه"},"b":{"en":"What you hear or read","tr":"Duyduğun ya da okuduğun","fa":"چیزی که می‌شنوی یا می‌خوانی"},"traps":{"en":"Traps","tr":"Tuzaklar","fa":"دام‌ها"},"example":{"en":"Example","tr":"Örnek","fa":"نمونه"},"ideas":{"en":"Ideas","tr":"Fikirler","fa":"ایده‌ها"}},"cards":[{"id":"x04-l4-ctx","exam":true,"patterns":["L4-society","PARA-signpost"],"often":{"pattern":"L4-society","n":10,"of":40,"label":{"en":"Comes up often in the real exam: 10 of the last 40 tests","tr":"Gerçek sınavda sık çıkar: son 40 sınavın 10’unda","fa":"در آزمون واقعی زیاد می‌آید: در ۱۰ آزمون از ۴۰ آزمونِ اخیر"}},"title":{"en":"A lecture on music in hospitals","tr":"Hastanelerde müzik üzerine bir ders","fa":"سخنرانی‌ای درباره‌ی موسیقی در بیمارستان"},"asks":{"en":"A lecturer explains why hospital waiting rooms are stressful, what research says about playing music in them, how the music should be chosen, the problems, and what may happen next.","tr":"Bir öğretim üyesi hastane bekleme salonlarının neden stresli olduğunu, oralarda müzik çalmanın etkileri hakkında araştırmaların ne dediğini, müziğin nasıl seçilmesi gerektiğini, sorunları ve sırada ne olabileceğini anlatıyor.","fa":"یک استاد توضیح می‌دهد چرا اتاق انتظار بیمارستان پراسترس است، پژوهش‌ها درباره‌ی پخش موسیقی در آن چه می‌گویند، موسیقی را چطور باید انتخاب کرد، مشکل‌ها چیست و بعد چه پیش می‌آید."},"fields":["Background","Effects on patients","Effects on staff","Choosing the music","Problems","The future"],"words":[{"a":"a source of","b":"something that causes","tr":"... kaynağı","fa":"منبعِ"},{"a":"anxiety","b":"worry / feeling nervous","tr":"kaygı","fa":"اضطراب"},{"a":"blood pressure","b":"the force of blood in the body","tr":"tansiyon","fa":"فشار خون"},{"a":"consistent","b":"the same each time","tr":"tutarlı","fa":"ثابت، یکدست"},{"a":"report","b":"say how they feel","tr":"bildirmek","fa":"گزارش دادن"},{"a":"distracting","b":"it takes your attention away","tr":"dikkat dağıtıcı","fa":"حواس‌پرت‌کن"},{"a":"outpatient","b":"a patient who does not stay the night","tr":"ayakta tedavi hastası","fa":"بیمار سرپایی"},{"a":"lyrics","b":"the words of a song","tr":"şarkı sözleri","fa":"شعرِ آهنگ"},{"a":"recognisable","b":"easy to know / familiar","tr":"tanıdık","fa":"آشنا، قابل‌تشخیص"},{"a":"licence","b":"official permission you pay for","tr":"lisans, izin belgesi","fa":"مجوز"},{"a":"free of charge","b":"without payment","tr":"ücretsiz","fa":"رایگان"},{"a":"headphones","b":"small speakers you wear on your ears","tr":"kulaklık","fa":"هدفون"}],"traps":[{"en":"\"You might expect... but\" and \"you might think... but\" bring in a wrong idea first. The note wants the idea after \"but\".","tr":"\"You might expect... but\" ve \"you might think... but\" önce yanlış bir fikri getirir. Not \"but\"tan sonraki fikri ister.","fa":"«You might expect... but» و «You might think... but» اول یک ایده‌ی غلط را می‌آورند. یادداشت ایده‌ی بعد از «but» را می‌خواهد."},{"en":"A word printed before the gap: \"a lower body ___\" needs one word. Do not repeat \"body\", and write the form you hear.","tr":"Boşluktan önce yazılı bir kelime: \"a lower body ___\" tek kelime ister. \"body\"yi tekrarlama ve duyduğun biçimi yaz.","fa":"کلمه‌ای که قبل از جای خالی چاپ شده: «a lower body ___» یک کلمه می‌خواهد. «body» را تکرار نکن و شکلی را که می‌شنوی بنویس."}],"example":{"text":"\"You might think bright colours would cheer patients up, but most prefer soft greens and blues.\"","item":"Patients prefer soft ___ (ONE WORD ONLY)","answer":"greens","why":{"en":"Bright colours are the wrong idea; after \"but\" comes the answer. Copy one word: greens (or blues).","tr":"Parlak renkler yanlış fikir; cevap \"but\"tan sonra geliyor. Tek kelime kopyala: greens (ya da blues).","fa":"رنگ‌های تند ایده‌ی غلط‌اند؛ جواب بعد از «but» می‌آید. یک کلمه کپی کن: greens (یا blues)."}}},{"id":"L4-society","ctx":["L4-society"],"title":{"en":"Society, health and business","tr":"Toplum, sağlık ve iş dünyası","fa":"جامعه، سلامت و کسب‌وکار"},"asks":{"en":"A presenter reports research on people: health, memory, music therapy, food trends, design for everyone or the workplace. The notes cover the problem, the study, the findings and what should change.","tr":"Bir sunucu insanlarla ilgili bir araştırmayı aktarır: sağlık, hafıza, müzik terapisi, yiyecek trendleri, herkes için tasarım ya da iş yeri. Notlar sorunu, çalışmayı, bulguları ve neyin değişmesi gerektiğini kapsar.","fa":"یک ارائه‌دهنده پژوهشی درباره‌ی آدم‌ها را گزارش می‌کند: سلامت، حافظه، موسیقی‌درمانی، روندهای غذایی، طراحی برای همه یا محیط کار. یادداشت‌ها مسئله، پژوهش، یافته‌ها و تغییرات لازم را پوشش می‌دهند."},"fields":["Background","Method","Participants","Findings","Benefits","Limitations","Recommendations"],"words":[{"a":"participants","b":"the people in the study / volunteers","tr":"katılımcılar","fa":"شرکت‌کننده‌ها"},{"a":"findings","b":"what the study showed","tr":"bulgular","fa":"یافته‌ها"},{"a":"therapy","b":"treatment","tr":"terapi","fa":"درمان"},{"a":"patients","b":"people in hospital","tr":"hastalar","fa":"بیماران"},{"a":"recovery","b":"getting better after an illness","tr":"iyileşme","fa":"بهبودی"},{"a":"stress","b":"feeling under pressure","tr":"stres","fa":"استرس"},{"a":"consumers","b":"shoppers / people who buy","tr":"tüketiciler","fa":"مصرف‌کننده‌ها"},{"a":"trend","b":"what's becoming popular","tr":"eğilim","fa":"روند"},{"a":"productivity","b":"how much work gets done","tr":"verimlilik","fa":"بهره‌وری"},{"a":"accessible","b":"easy for everyone to use","tr":"erişilebilir","fa":"قابل دسترس"},{"a":"elderly","b":"older people","tr":"yaşlılar","fa":"سالمندان"},{"a":"limitation","b":"what the study didn't look at","tr":"sınırlılık","fa":"محدودیت"},{"a":"recommend","b":"the speaker suggests","tr":"önermek","fa":"توصیه کردن"}],"traps":[{"en":"Check the group: \"older patients\" and \"all patients\" can have different answers.","tr":"Grubu kontrol et: \"yaşlı hastalar\" ile \"bütün hastalar\" farklı cevaplara sahip olabilir.","fa":"گروه را چک کن: «بیماران مسن» و «همه‌ی بیماران» ممکن است جواب‌های متفاوتی داشته باشند."},{"en":"Findings and limitations sound alike: \"the study showed X, but it didn’t look at Y\". Read the heading.","tr":"Bulgular ve sınırlılıklar birbirine benzer: \"çalışma X’i gösterdi ama Y’ye bakmadı\". Başlığı oku.","fa":"یافته‌ها و محدودیت‌ها شبیه هم‌اند: «پژوهش X را نشان داد، ولی Y را بررسی نکرد». عنوان را بخوان."}],"example":{"lines":[["Lecturer","Patients who listened to music before surgery needed less medication afterwards."],["Lecturer","Interestingly, the type of music made no difference."],["Lecturer","What mattered was that patients chose it themselves."]],"item":"Key factor: patients ___ the music themselves   (ONE WORD ONLY)","answer":"chose","why":{"en":"The type of music \"made no difference\". \"What mattered\" introduces the answer: chose.","tr":"Müziğin türü \"fark yaratmadı\". \"What mattered\" cevabı getiriyor: chose.","fa":"نوع موسیقی «فرقی نداشت». «What mattered» جواب را می‌آورد: chose."}},"general":true,"patterns":["L4-society"],"often":{"pattern":"L4-society","n":10,"of":40,"label":{"en":"Comes up often in the real exam: 10 of the last 40 tests","tr":"Gerçek sınavda sık çıkar: son 40 sınavın 10’unda","fa":"در آزمون واقعی زیاد می‌آید: در ۱۰ آزمون از ۴۰ آزمونِ اخیر"}}}]},"points":[{"eyebrow":{"en":"In the exam","tr":"Sınavda","fa":"در آزمون"},"head":{"en":"Ten notes, no break","tr":"On not, ara yok","fa":"ده یادداشت، بدون مکث"},"body":{"en":"Questions 31-40 are notes under six headings. You have about 45 seconds to read them, and the lecture then plays once without a break. Write ONE WORD ONLY in each gap.","tr":"Soru 31-40 altı başlık altındaki notlardır. Okumak için yaklaşık 45 saniyen var, sonra ders arasız bir kez çalınır. Her boşluğa ONE WORD ONLY yaz.","fa":"سؤال‌های ۳۱ تا ۴۰ یادداشت‌هایی زیر شش عنوان‌اند. حدود ۴۵ ثانیه برای خواندنشان وقت داری و بعد سخنرانی یک بار و بدون مکث پخش می‌شود. در هر جای خالی ONE WORD ONLY بنویس."}},{"eyebrow":{"en":"The method","tr":"Yöntem","fa":"روش"},"head":{"en":"Headings and signposts","tr":"Başlıklar ve yön gösteren ifadeler","fa":"عنوان‌ها و جمله‌های راهنما"},"steps":[{"en":"Match each heading to a signpost you expect: \"Let’s begin with\", \"Moving on to\", \"Turning to\", \"Finally\".","tr":"Her başlığı beklediğin bir yön gösterici ifadeyle eşleştir: \"Let’s begin with\", \"Moving on to\", \"Turning to\", \"Finally\".","fa":"هر عنوان را با جمله‌ی راهنمایی که انتظارش را داری جور کن: «Let’s begin with»، «Moving on to»، «Turning to»، «Finally»."},{"en":"Read the words before and after each gap and predict the kind of word: a feeling, a body part, a thing.","tr":"Her boşluktan önceki ve sonraki kelimeleri oku ve kelime türünü tahmin et: bir duygu, bir vücut bölümü, bir şey.","fa":"کلمه‌های قبل و بعد از هر جای خالی را بخوان و نوع کلمه را حدس بزن: یک احساس، یک عضو بدن، یک چیز."},{"en":"Write the word as you hear it: a plural stays plural, a comparative stays comparative.","tr":"Kelimeyi duyduğun gibi yaz: çoğul çoğul kalır, karşılaştırma biçimi de öyle kalır.","fa":"کلمه را همان‌طور که می‌شنوی بنویس: جمع جمع می‌ماند، صفت تفضیلی هم تفضیلی."}]},{"eyebrow":{"en":"The trap","tr":"Tuzak","fa":"دام"},"head":{"en":"The expected idea comes first","tr":"Beklenen fikir önce gelir","fa":"اول ایده‌ی مورد انتظار می‌آید"},"body":{"en":"Lecturers often say what you might expect before they give the research result. \"You might think older patients complain most, but...\" The answer is the result, not the expectation.","tr":"Öğretim üyeleri çoğu zaman araştırma sonucunu vermeden önce senin ne bekleyebileceğini söyler. \"En çok yaşlı hastaların şikâyet ettiğini düşünebilirsin, ama...\" Cevap beklenti değil, sonuçtur.","fa":"استادها اغلب قبل از گفتن نتیجه‌ی پژوهش می‌گویند شاید چه انتظاری داشته باشی. «شاید فکر کنی بیمارهای مسن بیشتر از همه شکایت می‌کنند، اما...». جواب نتیجه است، نه انتظار."}}],"example":{"eyebrow":{"en":"Worked example","tr":"Çözümlü örnek","fa":"مثال حل‌شده"},"head":{"en":"One note, step by step","tr":"Bir not, adım adım","fa":"یک یادداشت، قدم به قدم"},"instruction":"Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","item":"Hospital gardens · patients who can see trees need fewer ___","lines":[["Lecturer","In one study, patients whose windows looked out on trees asked for fewer painkillers than patients facing a wall."]],"answer":"painkillers","walk":[{"en":"\"Fewer\" before the gap means a plural noun.","tr":"Boşluktan önceki \"fewer\" çoğul bir isim gerektirir.","fa":"«fewer» قبل از جای خالی یعنی یک اسم جمع."},{"en":"\"Need\" in the note = \"asked for\" in the lecture.","tr":"Nottaki \"need\" = dersteki \"asked for\".","fa":"«need» در یادداشت یعنی «asked for» در سخنرانی."},{"en":"Write painkillers, plural, as you hear it.","tr":"Duyduğun gibi çoğul yaz: painkillers.","fa":"painkillers را جمع بنویس، همان‌طور که می‌شنوی."}]},"quick":{"prompt":"Note: \"the best lighting is ___\" (ONE WORD ONLY). You hear: \"You might expect bright light to be best, but in fact warm, soft light works better.\" What do you write?","options":["bright","soft","warm and soft"],"correct":1,"why":{"en":"Bright is the expected idea. After \"in fact\": warm, soft light. One word: soft (warm is also possible).","tr":"Parlak beklenen fikir. \"In fact\"ten sonra: sıcak, yumuşak ışık. Tek kelime: soft (warm da olabilir).","fa":"نور تند ایده‌ی مورد انتظار است. بعد از «in fact»: نور گرم و ملایم. یک کلمه: soft (warm هم ممکن است)."}},"remember":{"en":"Follow the signposts, wait for the result after \"but\" or \"in fact\", and write one word as you hear it.","tr":"Yön gösteren ifadeleri izle, \"but\" ya da \"in fact\"ten sonra sonucu bekle ve tek kelimeyi duyduğun gibi yaz.","fa":"جمله‌های راهنما را دنبال کن، بعد از «but» یا «in fact» منتظر نتیجه بمان و یک کلمه را همان‌طور که می‌شنوی بنویس."},"type":"explain","id":"c-x04-l4-e","course":"ielts-exams","exam":"exam-04","unit":"x04-l4"},"drill":{"type":"drill","skill":"listening","part":"Listening Part 4","title":{"en":"Music in hospital waiting rooms","tr":"Hastane bekleme salonlarında müzik","fa":"موسیقی در اتاق انتظار بیمارستان"},"timed":8,"ctx":["L4-society"],"script":[{"who":"Lecturer","line":"Good afternoon. Today, as part of our module on health environments, I want to look at something that most of us have experienced without thinking much about it: the music, or the silence, in hospital waiting rooms. It's a small detail of hospital design, but research suggests it may matter more than we expect."},{"who":"Lecturer","line":"Let's begin with the background. For many patients, the waiting room is one of the most stressful parts of a hospital visit. They're often worried about their results, they may be in pain, and they have no idea how long they'll have to wait. Surveys of patients regularly show that waiting rooms are a major source of anxiety, sometimes more so than the appointment itself. As long ago as the nineteenth century, nurses wrote about the calming effect of music on the sick, but it is only in the last few decades that scientists have tested the idea carefully."},{"who":"Lecturer","line":"So what happens when calm music is played? A number of studies have measured patients' physical responses, and the results are fairly consistent. When slow, gentle music is playing, patients tend to have a lower heart rate and lower blood pressure than patients in a silent room. They also report feeling calmer, although of course that's harder to measure. In some studies the difference appeared within about ten minutes of the music starting."},{"who":"Lecturer","line":"There's a more surprising finding, too. When researchers asked patients how long they thought they had waited, those who had listened to music usually guessed a shorter time than they had actually spent. In other words, music doesn't change the waiting time, but it makes the wait feel shorter."},{"who":"Lecturer","line":"Moving on to the staff. You might expect receptionists to find music distracting, but in one hospital that introduced music in its outpatient department, the reception staff reported fewer complaints from patients about delays. That's a real benefit, because dealing with angry patients is one of the most stressful parts of their job. Not everyone was happy, though. Some staff found that hearing the same songs all day was tiring, and the hospital had to make its playlists much longer. Nurses in the same department also noticed that patients seemed more willing to chat to each other while they waited."},{"who":"Lecturer","line":"Now, how should the music be chosen? The research gives us some fairly clear guidelines. The most calming music tends to be slow and steady, without sudden changes in volume. It's also best to choose music without lyrics, because people automatically try to follow the words, which keeps the mind busy rather than letting it rest. Volume matters too: the music should be quiet enough for people to hear their name when it is called."},{"who":"Lecturer","line":"Another guideline is to avoid well-known songs. You might think familiar music would be comforting, but popular songs often bring back memories, and those memories aren't always happy ones. In a hospital, where people may already be emotional, that can be upsetting. So most hospitals that use music choose pieces that are pleasant but not very recognisable."},{"who":"Lecturer","line":"Turning to the problems. The first is that not everyone wants music. A significant number of patients say they would prefer silence, especially if they're feeling unwell or trying to read. Others say music makes it harder to concentrate on the forms they have to fill in. One solution some hospitals have tried is to keep one area of the waiting room quiet, so that patients can choose where to sit."},{"who":"Lecturer","line":"The second problem is cost. Playing recorded music in a public space isn't free: organisations usually have to pay for licences, and for a large hospital with many waiting areas, the cost of those licences can add up. Some hospitals have got round this by working with local music students, who record pieces for them free of charge in exchange for experience."},{"who":"Lecturer","line":"Finally, what about the future? I think the most interesting development is personal choice. Several hospitals are testing systems in which patients choose their own music, using apps on their phones and their own headphones. That solves the silence problem, because those who prefer quiet can simply not use it. Researchers are also starting to study children's departments, where the right music, or perhaps sounds from nature, may help young patients in particular. And there is growing interest in the effect on staff over longer periods, since most studies so far have lasted only a few weeks."},{"who":"Lecturer","line":"For next week, please read the two articles on hospital lighting on the course website, because we'll be continuing this theme of how the environment affects patients."}],"notes":{"title":"Music in hospital waiting rooms","headings":["Background","Effects on patients","Effects on staff","Choosing the music","Problems","The future"]},"groups":[{"instruction":"Questions 31-40. Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","qtype":"note","questions":[{"n":31,"kind":"gap","prompt":"Background · surveys show waiting rooms are a major source of ___ for patients","accept":["anxiety"],"limit":1,"evidence":"waiting rooms are a major source of anxiety","why":{"en":"\"Waiting rooms are a major source of anxiety, sometimes more so than the appointment itself.\"","tr":"\"Bekleme salonları büyük bir kaygı kaynağı, bazen randevunun kendisinden bile fazla.\"","fa":"«اتاق انتظار منبع بزرگی از اضطراب است، گاهی بیشتر از خودِ ویزیت»."},"trap":["TR-paraphrase"]},{"n":32,"kind":"gap","prompt":"Effects on patients · calm music: a lower heart ___ and lower blood pressure","accept":["rate"],"limit":1,"evidence":"patients tend to have a lower heart rate","why":{"en":"\"A lower heart rate and lower blood pressure.\" \"heart\" is printed: one word.","tr":"\"Daha düşük kalp atış hızı ve tansiyon.\" \"heart\" yazılı: tek kelime.","fa":"«ضربان قلب و فشار خون پایین‌تر». «heart» چاپ شده: یک کلمه."},"trap":["TR-limit"]},{"n":33,"kind":"gap","prompt":"Effects on patients · the wait seems ___ than it really is","accept":["shorter"],"limit":1,"evidence":"makes the wait feel shorter","why":{"en":"Music \"doesn’t change the waiting time, but it makes the wait feel shorter\". Keep the comparative.","tr":"Müzik \"bekleme süresini değiştirmiyor ama beklemeyi daha kısa hissettiriyor\". Karşılaştırma biçimini koru.","fa":"موسیقی «زمان انتظار را عوض نمی‌کند، اما انتظار را کوتاه‌تر جلوه می‌دهد». صفت تفضیلی را نگه دار."},"trap":["TR-paraphrase","TR-limit"]},{"n":34,"kind":"gap","prompt":"Effects on staff · reception received fewer ___ from patients about delays","accept":["complaints"],"limit":1,"evidence":"the reception staff reported fewer complaints from patients about delays","why":{"en":"\"You might expect receptionists to find music distracting, but...\" they reported fewer complaints. Plural.","tr":"\"Resepsiyonistlerin müziği dikkat dağıtıcı bulmasını bekleyebilirsin ama...\" daha az şikâyet bildirdiler. Çoğul.","fa":"«شاید انتظار داشته باشی پذیرش موسیقی را حواس‌پرت‌کن بداند، اما...» شکایت کمتری گزارش کردند. جمع."},"trap":["TR-plural","TR-paraphrase"]},{"n":35,"kind":"gap","prompt":"Effects on staff · some found hearing the same songs all day ___","accept":["tiring"],"limit":1,"evidence":"hearing the same songs all day was tiring","why":{"en":"\"Some staff found that hearing the same songs all day was tiring.\"","tr":"\"Bazı çalışanlar bütün gün aynı şarkıları dinlemeyi yorucu buldu.\"","fa":"«بعضی کارکنان شنیدن آهنگ‌های تکراری در تمام روز را خسته‌کننده دیدند»."},"trap":["TR-paraphrase"]},{"n":36,"kind":"gap","prompt":"Choosing the music · best to choose music without ___","accept":["lyrics"],"limit":1,"evidence":"choose music without lyrics","why":{"en":"\"Choose music without lyrics, because people automatically try to follow the words.\"","tr":"\"Sözsüz müzik seç, çünkü insanlar istemeden sözleri takip etmeye çalışır.\"","fa":"«موسیقی بی‌کلام انتخاب کن، چون آدم‌ها ناخودآگاه سعی می‌کنند کلمه‌ها را دنبال کنند»."},"trap":["TR-plural"]},{"n":37,"kind":"gap","prompt":"Choosing the music · avoid well-known songs, which often bring back ___","accept":["memories"],"limit":1,"evidence":"popular songs often bring back memories","why":{"en":"\"You might think familiar music would be comforting, but popular songs often bring back memories.\" Plural.","tr":"\"Tanıdık müziğin rahatlatıcı olacağını düşünebilirsin ama popüler şarkılar çoğu zaman anıları geri getirir.\" Çoğul.","fa":"«شاید فکر کنی موسیقی آشنا آرامش می‌دهد، اما آهنگ‌های معروف اغلب خاطره‌ها را زنده می‌کنند». جمع."},"trap":["TR-plural","TR-paraphrase"]},{"n":38,"kind":"gap","prompt":"Problems · many patients say they would prefer ___","accept":["silence"],"limit":1,"evidence":"they would prefer silence","why":{"en":"\"A significant number of patients say they would prefer silence.\"","tr":"\"Hastaların önemli bir kısmı sessizliği tercih edeceklerini söylüyor.\"","fa":"«تعداد قابل‌توجهی از بیمارها می‌گویند سکوت را ترجیح می‌دهند»."},"trap":["TR-paraphrase"]},{"n":39,"kind":"gap","prompt":"Problems · the cost of ___ to play recorded music in public","accept":["licences"],"limit":1,"evidence":"organisations usually have to pay for licences","why":{"en":"\"Organisations usually have to pay for licences.\" Plural.","tr":"\"Kurumlar genelde lisanslar için ödeme yapmak zorunda.\" Çoğul.","fa":"«سازمان‌ها معمولاً باید هزینه‌ی مجوزها را بدهند». جمع."},"trap":["TR-plural"]},{"n":40,"kind":"gap","prompt":"The future · patients choose their own music through ___ on their phones","accept":["apps"],"limit":1,"evidence":"using apps on their phones","why":{"en":"\"Patients choose their own music, using apps on their phones and their own headphones.\"","tr":"\"Hastalar kendi müziklerini telefonlarındaki uygulamalarla ve kendi kulaklıklarıyla seçiyor.\"","fa":"«بیمارها موسیقی خودشان را با اپلیکیشن‌های گوشی و هدفون خودشان انتخاب می‌کنند»."},"trap":["TR-plural"]}]}],"audio":{"voices":{"Announcer":{"accent":"British (standard Southern)","gender":"M","tts":{"engine":"kokoro","voice":"bm_george"},"role":"Recorded rubric"},"Lecturer":{"accent":"North American","gender":"F","tts":{"engine":"kokoro","voice":"af_sarah"},"role":"Lecturer"}},"sequence":[{"type":"announcer","text":"Part 4. You will hear a lecture about music in hospital waiting rooms. First, you have some time to look at Questions 31 to 40."},{"type":"pause","seconds":45,"note":"reading time, Questions 31-40"},{"type":"announcer","text":"Now listen carefully and answer Questions 31 to 40."},{"type":"sfx","note":"lecture-room acoustics: slight reverb; 1.5 s between paragraphs"},{"type":"lines","from":0,"to":10},{"type":"announcer","text":"That is the end of Part 4. You now have two minutes to check your answers.","after":120}]},"id":"c-x04-l4-d","course":"ielts-exams","exam":"exam-04","unit":"x04-l4"},"exit":{"type":"exit","items":[{"tests":"word","point":1,"prompt":"The \"lyrics\" of a song are its...","options":["words","speed","instruments"],"correct":0,"why":{"en":"Lyrics = the words of a song.","tr":"Lyrics = bir şarkının sözleri.","fa":"lyrics یعنی شعر یا کلمه‌های آهنگ."}},{"tests":"paraphrase","point":1,"prompt":"Which phrase means \"causes worry\"?","options":["is a source of income","is a sign of progress","is a source of anxiety"],"correct":2,"why":{"en":"Anxiety = worry; \"a source of\" = something that causes it.","tr":"Anxiety = kaygı; \"a source of\" = ona yol açan şey.","fa":"anxiety یعنی نگرانی؛ «a source of» یعنی چیزی که باعثش می‌شود."}},{"tests":"method","point":2,"prompt":"The lecturer says \"Turning to the problems.\" What should you do?","options":["go back to the Background notes","move to the Problems heading","stop listening until the end"],"correct":1,"why":{"en":"A signpost tells you the next heading has begun.","tr":"Yön gösteren ifade bir sonraki başlığın başladığını söyler.","fa":"جمله‌ی راهنما می‌گوید عنوان بعدی شروع شده."}},{"tests":"trap","point":3,"prompt":"Note: \"visitors prefer ___ seating\" (ONE WORD ONLY). You hear: \"You might expect people to want armchairs, but most choose simple wooden benches.\" What do you write?","options":["wooden","armchair","simple wooden"],"correct":0,"why":{"en":"Armchairs are the expected idea; after \"but\": wooden benches. \"seating\" is printed, so one word: wooden.","tr":"Koltuklar beklenen fikir; \"but\"tan sonra: ahşap banklar. \"seating\" yazılı, tek kelime: wooden.","fa":"مبل ایده‌ی مورد انتظار است؛ بعد از «but»: نیمکت چوبی. «seating» چاپ شده، پس یک کلمه: wooden."}},{"tests":"trap","point":3,"prompt":"Note: \"the queue seems ___\" (ONE WORD ONLY). You hear: \"It makes the queue seem much shorter.\" What do you write?","options":["short","much shorter","shorter"],"correct":2,"why":{"en":"Keep the form you hear (shorter) and leave out \"much\" to stay in the limit.","tr":"Duyduğun biçimi koru (shorter) ve sınırda kalmak için \"much\"ı yazma.","fa":"شکلی را که می‌شنوی نگه دار (shorter) و برای ماندن در سقف کلمه «much» را ننویس."}}],"id":"c-x04-l4-x","course":"ielts-exams","exam":"exam-04","unit":"x04-l4"}}; })(globalThis.IELTS_EXAMS = globalThis.IELTS_EXAMS || {});
